title: Budget treasury spends per origin instead of per spend limit

doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      `pallet-treasury` limits how much an origin can approve within a single dispatch context, so
      that an origin cannot split one oversized spend across the calls of a batch. That running
      total was keyed by the origin's `max_amount` rather than by the origin, so two `SpendOrigin`
      variants configured with the same limit shared one budget and each reduced what the other
      could spend. Nothing enforced the assumption that every origin has a distinct limit, and a
      runtime upgrade could introduce the collision silently.

      The budget is now keyed by the SCALE-encoded origin caller, so each origin draws only on its
      own allowance. The key is the exact encoded origin, so two origin values that carry the same
      authority but different payloads, such as `Members(3, 5)` and `Members(4, 5)` from a
      collective, are budgeted separately. A single motion dispatches one origin value into every
      sub-call, so this only arises under Root `dispatch_as`.

      The same map was shared by every instance of the pallet, because the dispatch context keys
      values by type id and `SpendContext<BalanceOf<T, I>>` resolves to the same type for two
      instances using the same balance type. `SpendContext` is now generic over `T` and `I`, giving
      each instance its own map.

      No API change, and weights are unchanged: the map already existed and lives in memory for the
      duration of one dispatch.

crates:
  - name: pallet-treasury
    bump: patch
